# A count of a set that the system can grow (`supports two modes`) goes
# stale when someone adds a third, and nothing points the editor at the
# sentence. The fix drops the number (`supports these modes`) or names
# each item (`supports the single and all modes`).
#
# The rule counts a verb that states the count (`there are`,
# `supports`, `provides`, `offers`, `has`, `ships with`, `includes`,
# `defines`, `accepts`, or `comes with`), a number word from two to
# ten, at most one more word, and a noun for a set that can grow
# (`modes`, `backends`, `options`, `ways`). The noun list leaves out
# sets fixed by their nature (`ends`, `halves`, `parameters`). Weir
# cannot read a digit, so `supports 3 modes` passes. `the three modes`
# and `both backends` stay out, because in 12 of 14 sampled hits they
# pointed back at items the paragraph had just listed.
#
# It hit 61 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled hits
# counted a set that can grow. The misses counted a fixed pair (`two
# kinds of output: standard output and standard error`).
expr verbs [(there are), supports, provides, offers, has, (ships with), includes, defines, accepts, (comes with)]
expr count [two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten]
expr set [modes, backends, providers, drivers, plugins, formats, platforms, implementations, strategies, policies, profiles, levels, kinds, types, options, flags, subcommands, commands, endpoints, protocols, runtimes, engines, tiers, methods, algorithms, categories, versions, integrations, adapters, targets, architectures, ways]
expr main (@verbs @count [@set, (* @set)])
let message "Stale count. Drop the number, or name each item."
let description "Flags a count of a set that can grow, such as `supports two modes`, which goes stale when the set grows."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The API supports the single and all disruption modes."
allows "The function takes two parameters."
allows "Both backends read the same config."
